Z||000000||fotofrank||03-03-2001||02:39 PM|||| I some where heard that the cm , in an Iron Duke, can be replaced without taking the engine out. Is this true? the only way to do it would be to remove the right rear tire and fender welland 1/4 panel. Any other Ideas?<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||03-03-2001||05:04 PM||||There is a procedure for replacing the camshaft gear with the engine installed, but I don't think it would be possible to remove the actual camshaft.  Pulling the fender liner out wouldn't do the trick.  You would have to cut a rather large hole through the lower frame rail and the sheetmetal that forms part of the strut tower.  Not good structurally.  Much, much easier to drop the motor <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||03-04-2001||05:17 AM||||Hello everyone.<P>Actually you can. If you remove the lifters, which means taking off/out: pushrods, rocker arms, valve cover, egr (in way of valve cover), intake. Then drop only the back half of the cradle (remove rear cradle bolts, cradle will pivot on front cradle bolts). Tilting the cradle will allow you access to the timing cover. Oh yeah, remove the belts, wheel well inner liner, right rear tire. Remove timing cover and voila.<P>There's a good website describing the process. I don't have the address handy but check for a thread named "Fear of Timing Gear" it can be found there should you want it.||||reg||1
